# Line-ending policy.
#
# WHY THIS MATTERS HERE SPECIFICALLY
#
# This repository was developed on Windows, where the editor wrote CRLF into some files and LF into
# others — the sources are mixed. Without a policy, git normalises on commit in a way that depends on
# each developer's `core.autocrlf`, so a fresh clone on Linux can show the entire tree as modified,
# and a Windows contributor can accidentally commit a whole-file line-ending change.
#
# `text=auto` stores text in the repository as LF and checks it out using the platform default, which
# is the conventional choice: the repository has one representation and each platform gets its own
# working-tree form.
* text=auto

# Scripts that must keep LF even on Windows. The installer is executed directly by `npx`; a CRLF
# shebang line makes it unrunnable on POSIX systems.
*.js text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf

# Markdown keeps LF as well. It is mixed in this repository today; pinning it means a later edit
# cannot silently reflow an entire document as a line-ending diff.
*.md text eol=lf

# Binary: never touched, never diffed.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.tgz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary

# Keep GitHub's linguist from counting generated or vendored content in the language breakdown.
package-lock.json linguist-generated=true
pnpm-lock.yaml linguist-generated=true
